1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,560
The school moves from Saturday cardiac arrest into Sunday care of the
deteriorating patient, acute coronary syndrome, perfusion assessment
and live STEMI notification. This lesson follows that sequence rather
than adding an unrelated anatomy chapter.

2
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:31,272
A patient with a pulse may still be close to arrest. Your opportunity
is to identify the changing physiology and support the patient before
circulation fails, while arranging the help and receiving pathway the
situation needs.

3
00:00:31,272 --> 00:00:42,912
Use the previous rhythm and twelve-lead teaching as tools, not as a
reason to repeat a whole monitor chapter. Here the question is what
the trace and patient together require next.

4
00:00:42,912 --> 00:00:55,416
The original cases teach the school objectives without completing
school assessments. The original PCP PowerPoints and actual skill
rubric are not recovered, so their unseen instructions are not
invented.

5
00:00:55,416 --> 00:01:11,472
Peri-arrest describes unstable physiology around the time of an
arrest, including the period before it. New confusion, syncope,
ischemic discomfort, poor oxygenation or pulmonary edema can warn of
imminent deterioration.

6
00:01:11,472 --> 00:01:27,504
Use serial observations. A pressure of one hundred may represent a
major fall for this patient, while a slow rate can be well tolerated
in someone else. Neither a single cuff threshold nor a rhythm label
describes the whole circulation.

7
00:01:27,504 --> 00:01:39,672
A quieter patient is not automatically better. Exhaustion with smaller
breaths and declining awareness may be respiratory failure, even if
the earlier distress looked more dramatic.

8
00:01:39,672 --> 00:01:52,176
State the working problem aloud: deteriorating perfusion with a pulse,
or inadequate ventilation, for example. That statement aligns the team
before the exact diagnosis is settled.

9
00:01:52,176 --> 00:02:06,912
Approach safely and assess responsiveness, airway, breathing and
circulation. Correct an immediate threat with the least invasive
effective trained intervention; do not finish a history while
ventilation fails.

10
00:02:06,912 --> 00:02:20,712
Delegate tasks by actual capability. One partner can gather a focused
history while another obtains observations or prepares an ECG, as long
as someone continues to watch the patient and coordinate priorities.

11
00:02:20,712 --> 00:02:36,048
Bring movement equipment to the patient and minimize avoidable
exertion. Choose position according to breathing, perfusion, airway
protection and tolerance instead of requiring every cardiac patient to
sit or lie in one fixed angle.

12
00:02:36,048 --> 00:02:48,336
Make the conveyance and additional-resource decision early and keep
reassessing it. A needed intervention and a transport plan are
coordinated actions, not mutually exclusive choices.

13
00:02:48,336 --> 00:03:03,120
Coronary flow supplies the working myocardium. Plaque disruption with
thrombosis is a major acute mechanism, while spasm, severe anemia, low
flow and increased demand can create other supply-demand problems.

14
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:18,360
Pain, fear and sympathetic stimulation can increase rate, force and
vascular load. Calm communication and reducing exertion support care,
but reassurance alone cannot restore an occluded coronary artery.

15
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:30,696
Ischemia can impair both electrical stability and pumping. The patient
may develop a dangerous rhythm, pulmonary congestion, reduced output
or more than one complication at once.

16
00:03:30,696 --> 00:03:42,528
This is why medication pain relief is only one endpoint. You must
still follow perfusion, breathing, the ECG and the plan for definitive
evaluation or reperfusion.

17
00:03:42,528 --> 00:03:57,216
The school Week 5 cardiovascular video offers a broad disease-family
map. Use it to generate a differential, then return to the patient,
the ECG and current service guidance. It is not a bedside diagnosis
checklist.

18
00:03:57,216 --> 00:04:13,608
Congestion, pain, fatigue, syncope and abnormal pulses can arise
through different mechanisms. Ask what the finding means for flow,
oxygen supply, rhythm or cardiac filling rather than equating a
symptom with one disease name.

19
00:04:13,608 --> 00:04:28,968
Correct the video before memorizing it: PFO is not defined as a fixed
left-to-right shunt, and transposition is not caused by both fetal
connections failing to close. The bilingual guide gives the corrected
anatomy and references.

20
00:04:28,968 --> 00:04:42,120
Keep the operational layer separate. This lesson teaches recognition
and communication; current BCEHS pathways, actual authorization and
appropriate consultation determine field actions.

21
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:57,120
Acute coronary syndrome includes unstable angina, non-ST-elevation
infarction and ST-elevation infarction. Stable predictable angina is
not itself included, despite a contradictory early box in the main
text.

22
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:10,152
Symptoms alone usually cannot distinguish the acute categories. A
nondiagnostic ECG does not exclude ACS, and an NSTEMI can still have
significant ECG abnormalities.

23
00:05:10,152 --> 00:05:23,976
STEMI and NSTEMI are not reliable synonyms for a completely blocked
versus partly blocked artery in every patient. Use the actual clinical
and ECG pathway, not that simplified anatomical shortcut.

24
00:05:23,976 --> 00:05:37,464
The prehospital task is recognition, safe early treatment and
connection to the appropriate receiving service. Do not postpone those
actions until an unavailable biomarker result resolves the
terminology.

25
00:05:37,464 --> 00:05:54,264
Start with an open question and establish true onset and progression.
Chest pressure, breathlessness, nausea, weakness, diaphoresis or
syncope can be cardiac symptoms; do not require a classic clenched-
fist chest-pain story.

26
00:05:54,264 --> 00:06:07,296
Ask what was taken, how much and when, including dispatcher-assisted
ASA, home nitrate and medicines borrowed from another person. A listed
prescription is not proof of today’s dose.

27
00:06:07,296 --> 00:06:23,016
Ask sensitively about PDE5 medicines, explaining the possible
interaction. Include beta blockers, calcium-channel blockers,
anticoagulants, kidney disease and recent illness when these change
the risk or likely mechanism.

28
00:06:23,016 --> 00:06:37,008
Clarify an allergy by the actual reaction, and distinguish it from an
expected side effect. Obtain critical facts first; the remaining
history can continue without delaying urgent support or conveyance.

29
00:06:37,008 --> 00:06:53,016
Combine mentation, pulse quality, pressure, respiratory effort and
skin findings. Look for a pattern of low output, congestion or another
threat rather than accepting normal saturation as proof of normal
perfusion.

30
00:06:53,016 --> 00:07:07,464
Crackles, wheeze, neck-vein changes and edema provide useful context.
None independently proves a single diagnosis, and a patient can have
serious disease without all the expected findings.

31
00:07:07,464 --> 00:07:22,344
Assess relevant pulses and asymmetry without compressing both
carotids. A pulse-strength grade is descriptive, not an exact cuff
pressure. Do not keep repeating a difficult measurement while an
obvious primary threat goes untreated.

32
00:07:22,344 --> 00:07:35,352
Write down the actual findings and times. Rechecking after moving the
patient is especially important because exertion, position and
interrupted equipment can change the physiology and measurement.

33
00:07:35,352 --> 00:07:47,952
Chest symptoms are not automatically ACS. Aortic disease, pulmonary
embolism, pneumothorax and other conditions can resemble it and may
change the safety of a planned intervention.

34
00:07:47,952 --> 00:08:05,136
Use onset, associated neurologic or limb findings, breathing and
circulation to maintain a sensible differential. Equal arm pressures,
reproducible discomfort or a nondiagnostic ECG cannot individually
exclude the dangerous alternatives.

35
00:08:05,136 --> 00:08:18,024
Do not force every finding into one diagnosis. Chronic edema can
coexist with a new coronary problem, and a fast pulse may be
responding to blood loss rather than causing the illness.

36
00:08:18,024 --> 00:08:29,232
Report the working suspicion and important uncertainty early. Support
immediate needs while the receiving pathway provides investigations
that cannot be completed in the field.

37
00:08:29,232 --> 00:08:44,856
Use the Week Three landmark sequence and the actual trained equipment.
A twelve-lead recording uses ten electrodes. Limb or chest-lead
substitution can create misleading changes and cannot be repaired by a
confident interpretation.

38
00:08:44,856 --> 00:08:59,736
Position and expose respectfully, prepare the skin and reduce
avoidable movement or cable tension. Check the actual tracing for
baseline disturbance, disconnected leads and unusual patterns that
might reflect a placement problem.

39
00:08:59,736 --> 00:09:13,776
Record the symptom state and time, including whether treatment was
given before acquisition. A treatment-related change can matter, but
needed life support must not be withheld merely to preserve an
untreated ECG.

40
00:09:13,776 --> 00:09:24,960
If the trace is not interpretable, correct the likely cause promptly
while care continues. A printout with twelve labels is not
automatically a diagnostic recording.

41
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:39,936
The saved Week Three module defines its under-three-minute acquisition
drill from patient contact to a readable diagnostic ECG. Week Five
calls for timed acquisitions but does not provide a separate recovered
timing rubric.

42
00:09:39,936 --> 00:09:53,064
Carry the skill forward as a practice target, not as permission to
abandon immediate care. Assign tasks before the timer starts and keep
airway, ventilation and perfusion support running in parallel.

43
00:09:53,064 --> 00:10:06,264
Stop the practice clock at an actual usable recording, not the first
button press or first printed artifact. Record the delay reason
honestly and improve the workflow instead of changing the start time.

44
00:10:06,264 --> 00:10:19,152
If physiology deteriorates, the patient takes priority and the team
announces the change. A fast ECG that was obtained at the cost of
essential care is not a successful performance.

45
00:10:19,152 --> 00:10:32,616
The school asks you to recognize inferior STEMI rapidly. Inspect leads
Two, Three and a V F as a contiguous group, in a patient with
compatible symptoms and a technically useful recording.

46
00:10:32,616 --> 00:10:51,120
An illustrative criterion is new ST elevation of at least one
millimeter in two contiguous inferior leads in the appropriate
clinical setting. The more complex V Two and V Three thresholds belong
to the earlier lead-specific lesson, not a one-millimeter rule for
every lead.

47
00:10:51,120 --> 00:11:05,640
Look for reciprocal clues and evolving changes, but do not require a
reciprocal pattern before reporting concern. Mimics and baseline
abnormalities mean that the whole trace and clinical picture still
need trained interpretation.

48
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:19,320
Inferior ischemia can affect nodal function and coexist with right-
sided involvement. Slow rate and falling pressure are patient findings
to act on, not merely interesting additions to the ECG label.

49
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:35,664
Consider additional right-sided or posterior assessment through the
trained pathway when the clinical and standard ECG findings suggest
it. Extra lead positions must be accurate and clearly labeled, not
mixed with a standard recording without explanation.

50
00:11:35,664 --> 00:11:50,448
An inferior pattern alone does not prove right ventricular infarction.
Conversely, a single absent clue does not erase concern in a
hypotensive patient. Integrate the trace, perfusion and medication
screen.

51
00:11:50,448 --> 00:12:03,816
Do not keep delaying notification until every possible lead has been
obtained. Send or describe the useful information already available,
explain what remains uncertain and coordinate further acquisition if
appropriate.

52
00:12:03,816 --> 00:12:16,800
A lead-based prediction is not a substitute for ongoing patient
assessment. Changes in consciousness, breathing or pulse still drive
immediate priorities even while the ECG work continues.

53
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:31,896
Give a concise identity and time sequence: onset or last symptom-free
time when known, first contact, ECG time and expected arrival.
Distinguish confirmed facts from estimates or collateral reports.

54
00:12:31,896 --> 00:12:46,560
State suspected STEMI territory together with the patient state, such
as inferior changes with bradycardia and hypoperfusion. Include
relevant treatment, actual response and why a usual intervention was
withheld.

55
00:12:46,560 --> 00:13:02,760
The school case asks for a PCI-center bypass pre-alert. In real
practice, eligibility, geography, instability and regional agreements
determine the destination. Do not announce a universal bypass without
confirming the actual pathway.

56
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:16,824
Transmit an interpretable trace where the system supports it and
confirm receipt or the next communication step. A sent message is not
proof that a cath team accepted activation or that responsibility has
transferred.

57
00:13:16,824 --> 00:13:31,344
ASA inhibits platelet cyclooxygenase irreversibly and reduces
thromboxane activity. Its purpose in appropriate suspected ACS is not
analgesic proof, venodilation or direct clot dissolution.

58
00:13:31,344 --> 00:13:45,576
Check prior intake, the actual allergy reaction and relevant bleeding
concerns. ASA-triggered respiratory reaction matters, but an asthma
diagnosis alone is not proof that the patient is allergic to ASA.

59
00:13:45,576 --> 00:14:03,888
The saved BC examination guide specifies one hundred sixty milligrams;
the checked current service monograph specifies one hundred sixty-two
milligrams chewed and swallowed. The main text has a wider older
range. Keep the required source explicit rather than combining doses.

60
00:14:03,888 --> 00:14:17,592
Follow the applicable protocol for prior doses and contraindications.
The patient still needs monitoring, reassessment and appropriate
conveyance after ASA; a tablet cannot complete the coronary pathway.

61
00:14:17,592 --> 00:14:29,832
A patient may have inadequate tissue oxygen delivery from poor flow or
anemia despite a reassuring saturation. A low-quality peripheral
signal during shock may also be unreliable.

62
00:14:29,832 --> 00:14:44,088
Provide oxygen according to the applicable clinical guidance and the
patient’s need, while assessing ventilation and perfusion. Increasing
oxygen flow cannot replace ventilating a patient whose breaths are
inadequate.

63
00:14:44,088 --> 00:14:57,912
The older Nancy ninety-to-ninety-four-percent band differs from
checked current local guidance. Keep these source targets labeled; do
not invent a universal average or a rigid cap that prevents correction
of hypoxemia.

64
00:14:57,912 --> 00:15:13,704
Post-ROSC hyperoxia precautions are covered in the separate arrest-
transition lecture. They do not justify withholding needed oxygen
during active resuscitation or ignoring a deteriorating patient
because one saturation appears normal.

65
00:15:13,704 --> 00:15:27,408
At usual lower doses, nitroglycerin predominantly dilates veins and
reduces preload. This may relieve selected ischemic discomfort but can
reduce output when the circulation depends on limited filling.

66
00:15:27,408 --> 00:15:42,936
The checked current PCP monograph requires systolic pressure at least
one hundred ten and rate between fifty and one hundred fifty, with no
significant hypoperfusion. Passing the numbers alone does not clear
the other contraindications.

67
00:15:42,936 --> 00:15:58,632
Ask about sildenafil or vardenafil within twenty-four hours and
tadalafil within forty-eight hours under that monograph. These
medicines may be used for more than erectile dysfunction, so ask
respectfully and explain the interaction.

68
00:15:58,632 --> 00:16:16,848
The contraindication list also includes right-sided acute infarction,
uncorrected hypovolemia, tamponade and other specific conditions.
Inferior ischemia requires caution and assessment for right-sided
involvement; pain severity does not override the screen.

69
00:16:16,848 --> 00:16:30,360
Follow the serial circuit. If right-sided filling or pumping is
already limited, less blood may reach the left ventricle. Additional
venodilation can reduce filling further and worsen systemic output.

70
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:45,408
The school case combines inferior changes, a rate of forty-four and a
pressure of eighty-eight over fifty. Both the pressure and rate fail
the current PCP nitrate screen, even before the additional concern
about right-sided involvement.

71
00:16:45,408 --> 00:17:01,296
Do not simplify this into every inferior infarct is identical or every
patient needs a fluid bolus. Congestion, volume status, breathing and
the likely mechanism still determine the appropriate support and
consultation.

72
00:17:01,296 --> 00:17:15,816
Withhold an excluded intervention, explain why, protect immediate
physiology and arrange appropriate help and conveyance. A
contraindication is a decision to choose safer care, not a decision to
do nothing.

73
00:17:15,816 --> 00:17:30,936
The current monograph lists a point-four-milligram sublingual dose at
three-to-five-minute intervals; C Zero One names four to five minutes.
These are an identified source difference, not a new combined interval
invented by this course.

74
00:17:30,936 --> 00:17:44,472
The same current monograph requires prior CliniCall consultation
without a prior prescription or when more than three doses are needed.
The older main-text three-to-six-dose wording does not remove that
requirement.

75
00:17:44,472 --> 00:17:58,368
Check pressure, rate, actual perfusion and relevant symptoms before
every authorized repeat. A previous tolerated dose does not guarantee
safety after volume loss, rhythm change or a falling pressure.

76
00:17:58,368 --> 00:18:09,840
Reassess pain and adverse effects without using headache or sublingual
burning as a potency test. Do not remain on scene merely to finish a
fixed number of symptom-relief doses.

77
00:18:09,840 --> 00:18:24,624
Hypotension can arise through several mechanisms. Assess volume loss,
congestion, pump function clues, obstruction, medicines and the
clinical trend before assuming the ventricle simply needs more fluid.

78
00:18:24,624 --> 00:18:38,400
An indicated fluid intervention needs a patient-specific authorized
plan and a response check. In suspected cardiogenic shock, the current
shock guideline recommends consultation for care planning before
treatment.

79
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:51,600
Watch mentation, pulse, pressure, breathing and congestion, not only
the amount that entered the line. More volume can improve filling or
make lung function worse depending on the underlying problem.

80
00:18:51,600 --> 00:19:04,392
Establish access when appropriate without delaying definitive care. A
patent IV is preparation, not a requirement to give a large bolus or
evidence that the cause has been corrected.

81
00:19:04,392 --> 00:19:17,184
A low rate may be normal for an individual, medication-related or a
sign of disease. Assess the patient’s baseline, symptoms and perfusion
rather than treating every rate below sixty.

82
00:19:17,184 --> 00:19:32,184
Current C Zero Two uses a below-fifty framework and emphasizes
clinical endpoints rather than a target number alone. A relatively
well-perfused patient may need monitoring and conveyance without
immediate rate-specific treatment.

83
00:19:32,184 --> 00:19:45,360
Search for hypoxia, ischemia, vagal influence, medicines or overdose,
kidney disease and electrolyte risk. Correcting a cause can be more
important than making a monitor rate look normal.

84
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:55,704
Continue to watch for a changing state. Stable describes the present
assessment, not a guarantee about the next ten minutes or permission
to leave the patient unobserved.

85
00:19:55,704 --> 00:20:09,504
A slow rhythm with worsening mentation, hypotension or other
hypoperfusion is a different clinical state. Provide effective primary
support and request the appropriate advanced help without waiting for
complete rhythm certainty.

86
00:20:09,504 --> 00:20:25,800
Atropine, epinephrine infusion and transcutaneous pacing are advanced
service interventions, not independent PCP actions created by this
lesson. Preparation and assistance still matter, including equipment
and accurate observations.

87
00:20:25,800 --> 00:20:41,424
The main text lists atropine point-five milligrams, the PHARM
candidate one milligram, and the checked local monograph point-six
milligrams for adult bradycardia. Keep those source regimens separate
instead of choosing or averaging a dose.

88
00:20:41,424 --> 00:20:57,792
In high-grade block or severe peri-arrest physiology, a drug may not
provide adequate improvement and advanced electrical or perfusion
support may be needed. A faster ECG without effective circulation is
not a successful endpoint.

89
00:20:57,792 --> 00:21:07,536
Tachycardia may compensate for a reduced stroke volume or other
stress. Rate reduction without treating that cause can make output and
perfusion worse.

90
00:21:07,536 --> 00:21:22,536
Ask whether the illness preceded the rhythm or the symptoms began with
it, and integrate the actual ECG and patient findings. This is
reasoning under uncertainty, not a perfect history test that must
delay primary support.

91
00:21:22,536 --> 00:21:34,368
A rate below one hundred fifty does not guarantee safety. The type of
rhythm, underlying disease and patient response matter more than
assuming one universal dangerous-rate threshold.

92
00:21:34,368 --> 00:21:44,928
If effective circulation disappears, stop using the with-a-pulse
pathway. State the transition clearly and begin the appropriate arrest
response with the team.

93
00:21:44,928 --> 00:21:57,336
The previous manual-defibrillation lesson taught the arrest shock
workflow. A patient with a pulse has different electrical-treatment
branches that require trained advanced authority and patient-specific
decisions.

94
00:21:57,336 --> 00:22:08,184
Synchronized cardioversion coordinates energy to a detected
ventricular complex in a suitable rhythm. It is not a smaller shock
that can be substituted whenever a pulse is present.

95
00:22:08,184 --> 00:22:21,816
Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia may not permit reliable
synchronization and can require unsynchronized defibrillation. Do not
delay urgent treatment trying indefinitely to obtain synchronization
markers.

96
00:22:21,816 --> 00:22:34,704
Pacing requires both electrical and mechanical assessment under the
actual procedure. A spike or displayed rate is not proof of capture
with effective perfusion, and this overview is not a pacing checkoff.

97
00:22:34,704 --> 00:22:47,328
Fluid in lung tissue or alveoli can impair gas exchange and increase
breathing work. Cardiac dysfunction is important, but toxic exposure
and drowning are among other causes.

98
00:22:47,328 --> 00:22:58,920
Assess the whole pattern instead of diagnosing from wheeze alone.
Congestion, history, pressure and other findings can help, while mixed
lung and heart disease can coexist.

99
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:13,464
A patient who cannot tolerate lying flat should not be forced flat
merely for a convenient examination. At the same time, falling
pressure or impaired airway protection may change the safe position
and support plan.

100
00:23:13,464 --> 00:23:27,312
Diminishing breath size, falling awareness or an inability to maintain
ventilation signals failure, not improvement. Be ready to assist
ventilation effectively, especially during movement or a positional
change.

101
00:23:27,312 --> 00:23:43,512
CPAP can help selected spontaneously breathing patients, but a
pulmonary-edema label does not automatically make it appropriate.
Apply the actual training, indication and exclusion criteria from the
earlier airway course and current procedure.

102
00:23:43,512 --> 00:23:56,352
The checked procedure requires an awake patient able to follow
commands and maintain an airway, and excludes important unsafe states.
It is not a replacement for ventilation in apnea or inadequate
breathing.

103
00:23:56,352 --> 00:24:10,416
Observe work of breathing, tidal volume, alertness, oxygenation,
pressure and tolerance. Positive pressure can affect venous return, so
the circulation must be reassessed along with the lungs.

104
00:24:10,416 --> 00:24:25,032
If the patient fails, change to effective BVM support and appropriate
escalation rather than simply tightening a mask or raising pressure.
Use a PEEP valve only when its actual procedure and patient conditions
permit.

105
00:24:25,032 --> 00:24:39,288
Obstruction can prevent filling or forward ejection even when
myocardium is not the primary failure. Tamponade and major pulmonary
embolism illustrate why more rate or a pain medicine may not solve the
problem.

106
00:24:39,288 --> 00:24:52,632
Beck’s triad, electrical alternans or pulsus paradoxus may suggest
tamponade, but their absence cannot exclude it. No single classical
sign provides certainty in every patient.

107
00:24:52,632 --> 00:25:08,232
Combine history, breathing, perfusion and available findings, and
communicate the suspected mechanism. Do not delay urgent support while
searching for every classic sign or assume equal breath sounds exclude
all other threats.

108
00:25:08,232 --> 00:25:19,944
Definitive invasive treatment depends on the cause and advanced or
surgical resources. Reading about a needle or thrombolytic does not
authorize an independent PCP procedure.

109
00:25:19,944 --> 00:25:33,528
Shock involves inadequate tissue oxygen delivery or use in context.
The familiar four mechanisms help organize reasoning, but patients may
have mixed disease and a normal pressure during early compensation.

110
00:25:33,528 --> 00:25:45,000
Follow the brain, breathing, pulse and skin along with pressure. A
single higher reading after treatment is not proof that organ
perfusion or the underlying cause improved.

111
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:57,600
Cardiogenic shock does not require a known percentage of infarcted
muscle. Ischemia, rhythm, toxic effects or mechanical problems can
reduce output through different pathways.

112
00:25:57,600 --> 00:26:11,928
Describe the likely mechanism, immediate needs and uncertainty when
requesting help. The next intervention must match that reasoning and
actual authority, rather than treating every shock with the same large
bolus.

113
00:26:11,928 --> 00:26:23,928
Dissection creates a false channel in the aortic wall; aneurysm is
abnormal dilation and can leak or rupture. They are different
disorders, and either can cause a time-critical patient.

114
00:26:23,928 --> 00:26:36,576
Abrupt maximal pain, syncope or neurologic and limb findings are
important clues. Some patients do not have classic tearing pain, and
equal arm pressures do not rule out dissection.

115
00:26:36,576 --> 00:26:51,552
Reduce exertion, support the patient and communicate the suspicion
promptly for the appropriate destination. Fluid, analgesia and
pressure management require the actual cause-specific plan rather than
copying the ACS pathway.

116
00:26:51,552 --> 00:27:05,928
Do not start a generic antihypertensive or chase a normal pressure
independently from this reading. A leaking aneurysm is a surgical
emergency, not an opportunity to finish every possible scene procedure
first.

117
00:27:05,928 --> 00:27:18,192
Pain, distress and technique can affect a pressure measurement. Repeat
a sound measurement while assessing the patient, rather than
diagnosing chronic hypertension from one emergency reading.

118
00:27:18,192 --> 00:27:33,168
Hypertensive emergency involves acute target-organ injury. Neurologic
change, acute pulmonary edema, ischemia and aortic disease have
different treatment considerations; the number alone does not identify
the pathway.

119
00:27:33,168 --> 00:27:47,880
Do not apply the older textbook single MAP threshold or one-third
reduction instruction as a universal field rule. Rapid indiscriminate
lowering can harm perfusion, especially when a specific condition
needs a different approach.

120
00:27:47,880 --> 00:28:00,312
Provide indicated support and timely appropriate evaluation, and
communicate the actual observations and trend. Chronic follow-up and
an acute emergency are distinct decisions.

121
00:28:00,312 --> 00:28:13,848
A fifty-nine-year-old at a community meeting develops chest pressure
and nausea. The patient is clammy, has a rate of forty-six and a
pressure of eighty-six over fifty-two, and currently breathes
adequately.

122
00:28:13,848 --> 00:28:31,608
State the problem as suspected acute coronary ischemia with
bradycardia and poor perfusion. Support immediate needs, minimize
exertion, prepare equipment and appropriate resources, and obtain or
preserve the useful ECG without delaying urgent care.

123
00:28:31,608 --> 00:28:44,088
The nitrate screen fails. Explain pressure and rate exclusions plus
possible right-sided involvement; do not give a dose merely because a
family member hands you the patient’s usual spray.

124
00:28:44,088 --> 00:28:59,856
Pre-alert with the timeline, inferior findings, perfusion, actual
actions and response, and confirm the regional destination plan. This
original exercise tests reasoning, not recall of the school patient’s
age or answer wording.

125
00:28:59,856 --> 00:29:14,112
First change only the pressure to one hundred sixteen while the rate
remains forty-six. Nitrate is still not automatically appropriate. One
improved number cannot erase the other failed criteria or right-sided
concern.

126
00:29:14,112 --> 00:29:28,512
Next keep a pulse but make the breaths shallow and awareness fall.
Reassess the airway and provide effective ventilatory support with the
team; a normal-looking oxygen saturation is not permission to ignore
inadequate ventilation.

127
00:29:28,512 --> 00:29:44,640
Then change pulse status to absent with unresponsiveness and abnormal
breathing. Announce the transition and begin the arrest response,
including CPR and the appropriate analysis workflow, rather than
continuing a with-a-pulse medication discussion.

128
00:29:44,640 --> 00:29:58,152
Finally restore a perfusing state in the drill. Reassess and enter the
verified post-arrest pathway; do not simply return to the original
chest-pain checklist as though the arrest never occurred.

129
00:29:58,152 --> 00:30:13,224
Document the actual history, observations and trace times, including
changes and measurement limitations. Do not allow an automatic monitor
event or imported vital sign to replace your assessment of effective
circulation.

130
00:30:13,224 --> 00:30:24,432
Record what was given, what was not given and why. A withheld
contraindicated nitrate is a reasoned safety action, not a blank
medication field that should be concealed.

131
00:30:24,432 --> 00:30:38,904
Use a concise clinical handover to transfer the current state,
suspected problem, relevant background, response and tasks still
needed. Tell the receiving team about recurrent symptoms or
deterioration during the journey.

132
00:30:38,904 --> 00:30:49,368
Confirm the handover and maintain observation until responsibility
transfers appropriately. The pre-alert starts coordination; it does
not end your patient care.

133
00:30:49,368 --> 00:31:35,256
The actual first range includes the opening of 17.4. Potassium-channel
effects can prolong repolarization and QT, while the QT itself also
changes with heart rate. QTc is a rate-corrected estimate using a
named formula; the chapter mentions Bazett, but no formula or one
automatic monitor number removes the need to assess the tracing, rate,
medicines and patient. Amiodarone has effects across several
channel/receptor groups and can persist long after chronic therapy
stops. A long elimination time is not permission to confuse its arrest
push with a perfusing-rhythm infusion. This boundary does not assign
every later class-III drug table.

134
00:31:35,256 --> 00:32:23,568
The final assigned page opens 22.3, moving from cardiac drugs to
shock. In anaphylaxis, airway narrowing and vasodilation can impair
oxygen delivery together. Epinephrine supports bronchial and vascular
responses through different receptors; the hospital text mentioning IM
or IV does not make those routes freely interchangeable. Current E09
uses IM epinephrine first for systemic anaphylaxis and reserves IV
treatment for the relevant advanced refractory pathway. There are no
absolute epinephrine contraindications in life-threatening anaphylaxis
in the current monograph. An arrest-dose IV bolus is not the dose to
copy into a patient who still has a pulse.

135
00:32:23,568 --> 00:33:09,072
Translate the ending monitoring list into parallel care: reassess
breathing effort, air entry, saturation, pressure, consciousness, ECG
where available and the clinical response, with rescue equipment
ready. Do not delay indicated IM epinephrine while obtaining a perfect
monitor setup. Ask a conscious patient to report better or worse
breathing or other change; inability to report makes observation more
important. The next albuterol heading begins but continues beyond the
assigned page. Salbutamol can support persistent bronchospasm after
epinephrine in the current pathway; it does not replace epinephrine or
correct the entire anaphylactic circulation problem.

136
00:33:09,072 --> 00:33:56,736
Follow the medication through an error-sensitive chain: prescribing,
transcription, dispensing, preparation, administration, monitoring and
recording. A complete instruction includes the correct patient,
generic or verified product name, dose with units, necessary strength
or concentration, route, timing, indication and valid prescriber or
protocol authority. Date, time and authentication matter for a written
or verbal order. A chemical name describes structure, a generic name
identifies the active agent and a brand identifies a marketed product;
none alone proves the formulation. Resolve an incomplete order before
giving it, even when the drug cabinet or computer accepted it.

137
00:33:56,736 --> 00:34:55,824
Decode a route without silently choosing it. PO, SL and buccal refer
to oral, under-tongue and cheek placement; PR and PV refer to rectal
and vaginal routes. IM, IV, IV push, IV piggyback, SC or SQ and ID
name different tissue or vascular routes. Inhaled, nasal, topical,
transdermal and nasogastric delivery are also different. The source
uses AD/AS/AU for ears and OD/OS/OU for eyes; ambiguous shorthand is
unsafe. Its EPI entry means epidural, not automatically epinephrine,
while IA, IC and IT mean intra-arterial, intracardiac and intrathecal.
Recognizing those labels is not permission to use the route. Clarify
unfamiliar abbreviations and write the actual medicine and route
clearly.

138
00:34:55,824 --> 00:35:30,360
Use a medicine name as a clue, then verify the actual product.
Metoprolol is a beta blocker, lisinopril is an ACE inhibitor, and
losartan is an angiotensin receptor blocker. Those are different ways
to influence the circulation, not interchangeable tablets for a low or
high pressure. The bilingual reader gives the longer spelling
reference. In the weekly case, explain what the confirmed medicine
could change in rate, contraction, filling, resistance or potassium,
and check the patient rather than selecting a drug from its ending.

139
00:35:30,360 --> 00:36:10,008
The remaining reference groups medicines for infection, inflammation,
allergy, airway narrowing, pain, seizures, sleep and mental health.
Recognizing a family helps anticipate questions about effects and
interactions, but it does not establish the exact medicine, approved
indication, formulation, route or dose. Some matching name endings
have important exceptions. Two medicines affecting one organ may be
intentionally prescribed together. Compare the real plan and the
reason for each treatment instead of declaring every such combination
an error or turning the reference table into a medication order.

140
00:36:10,008 --> 00:36:54,624
Action and indication answer different questions: how the drug works
versus why it is prescribed. Beta blockers reduce receptor responses;
they do not simply stop adrenaline being released. Diuretics can
change renal salt and water handling and reduce circulating volume, so
the patient with bradycardia and falling pressure needs more than a
medicine-name checklist. An FDA-labelled or off-label example is U.S.
regulatory context, not a BC paramedic authority statement. Compare
the real indication, interactions and expected response. The source
statement that twice daily automatically means every twelve hours must
not override the actual prescribed schedule.

141
00:36:54,624 --> 00:37:38,184
Build the safe environment around the high-alert medicine. Keep
products secure, prepare for one identified patient at a time, label
every prepared container and protect against interruptions, loss and
accidental reuse. Do not put an unidentified syringe or patient
medicine in a pocket. Tall Man lettering highlights risky differences,
such as DOPamine versus DOBUTamine or hydrALAZINE versus hydrOXYzine;
it is not an identity check by itself. An independent second check and
a read-back address different errors. Apply the actual agency storage,
controlled-drug accounting and documentation process rather than
importing a U.S. ward rule as BC law.

142
00:37:38,184 --> 00:38:32,064
Separate an expected side effect, an unwanted adverse reaction, immune
allergy, excessive toxic exposure, tolerance, interaction and an
apparently opposite response. They are not interchangeable allergy
labels. Tolerance means a weaker response after repeated exposure; it
is not the same as physical dependence or addiction and does not
authorize a self-selected dose increase. A new symptom could instead
be worsening disease, a wrong product or a delivery error. Support
immediate threats, assess the actual reaction and report facts and
timing. Finish 11.1 with history/assessment, interaction checks,
understandable education and the informed refusal process under the
real consent framework. The source note about U.S. parental rights is
not automatically a BC rule.

143
00:38:32,064 --> 00:39:17,616
The literal 18.4 pages add selected cardiopulmonary support to the
examination. Diaphragmatic and pursed-lip techniques may help a
cooperative patient control breathing, while coughing, deep breaths,
incentive spirometry, repositioning and appropriate mobility support
selected ongoing-care plans. An incentive spirometer is an inhalation
exercise device, not an oxygen source or a rescue ventilator. Do not
label severe breathlessness as anxiety or delay airway/perfusion
support while coaching. Evaluate the patient, the indication,
technique and response; an anecdote about a postoperative patient is
not a universal prescription for a near-arrest patient.

144
00:39:17,616 --> 00:40:06,072
Connect the medication tables to mechanisms already taught this week.
Bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle; anti-inflammatory
treatment is different from immediate bronchodilation, secretion-
modifying drugs differ from cough suppression, and antimicrobials
require an appropriate infection indication. Cardiac drugs may alter
rate, conduction, contraction, loading, lipids or thrombosis. A beta
blocker does not automatically have amiodarone-like class-III effects;
an anticoagulant does not directly replace an antiplatelet. Compare
the actual product and contraindications, then observe ventilation and
perfusion. The hospital table is not a set of additional independent
PCP medication permissions.

145
00:40:06,072 --> 00:40:55,176
Choose oxygen delivery by the actual need and device, not a promise of
one exact percentage. The source tables give nasal-cannula, simple-
mask, reservoir-mask and higher-support examples; flow and inspired
concentration depend on fit, design and patient breathing.
Conventional oxygen does not provide a breath to an apneic patient.
High-flow nasal systems, noninvasive positive pressure, a bag-mask and
an invasive ventilator are not interchangeable. Use the separately
labelled current target, observe breathing/pressure and reassess. A
patient with COPD is not automatically denied needed oxygen, and a
high saturation does not establish adequate ventilation or cardiac
output.

146
00:40:55,176 --> 00:41:44,208
Understand what the hospital interventions change. A chest drain
removes pleural air or fluid through a prescribed system; it is not a
reason to clamp or disconnect a transferred drain routinely.
Electrical cardioversion, arrest defibrillation, pacing and
airway/ventilator support have different purposes and authorization.
After movement or an intervention, recheck the patient and the actual
connections, delivery and response. Mechanical ventilation introduces
infection, pressure and sedation-related considerations. The source
recommendation about a proton-pump inhibitor preventing ventilator-
associated pneumonia must not become routine VAP prophylaxis: stress-
ulcer prevention and pneumonia prevention are different decisions.

147
00:41:44,208 --> 00:42:35,088
Finish the management pages with continuity, not just machinery.
Explain the actual care plan, use teach-back and include the patient
preferences and relevant supporters. Smoking/pollutant exposure,
nutrition, energy conservation, cardiac rehabilitation, coping and
comfort need individualized plans and appropriate team members. Chest
physiotherapy or postural drainage is not an automatic response to
every crackle, and diet counselling cannot displace immediate
stabilization. In the weekly slow, hypotensive chest-pain case,
describe the treatment purpose and a patient-level response, identify
an adverse change and hand over what was actually done. This links the
medication-rights, cardiopulmonary-management and cardiovascular-
assessment readings into one encounter.

148
00:42:35,088 --> 00:42:46,920
Explain one whole encounter from first contact to the receiving team.
Start with immediate threats and show how the history, observations
and ECG change your next actions.

149
00:42:46,920 --> 00:42:59,904
Explain the Nitro Trap through venous return and the serial circuit,
then state the actual pressure, rate and other screening failures. Do
not reduce it to a slogan that all inferior infarcts are the same.

150
00:42:59,904 --> 00:43:13,392
Separate source differences and operator authority from physiology.
You should understand advanced treatments well enough to recognize and
assist appropriately without claiming permissions that the course does
not grant.

151
00:43:13,392 --> 00:43:24,792
Use the bilingual CNS, PHARM and Nancy reading below to reconstruct
the reasoning, then practise the original questions and move into the
integrated weekend team rehearsal.
